  "text": "𬜯\n\n#### Step 1: Simplify the Logarithmic Expressions\n\nWe start by simplifying each term in the product using the change of base formula for logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_k(5^{k^2-1}) = (k^2 - 1) \\log_k(5)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4}) = (k^2 - 4) \\log_{k+1}(5)\n\\]\nWe can rewrite the product as:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\log_k(5)}{(k^2 - 4) \\log_{k+1}(5)}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 2: Express Terms in Terms of the Same Base\n\nNext, we express the terms in the product in terms of the same base. Notice that:\n\\[\n\\log_k(5) = \\frac{\\log_5(5)}{\\log_5(k)} = \\frac{1}{\\log_5(k)}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{k+1}(5) = \\frac{\\log_5(5)}{\\log_5(k+1)} = \\frac{1}{\\log_5(k+1)}\n\\]\nThus, the product becomes:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\frac{1}{\\log_5(k)}}{(k^2 - 4) \\frac{1}{\\log_5(k+1)}} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\log_5(k+1)}{(k^2 - 4) \\log_5(k)}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 3: Simplify the Expression\n\nWe can rewrite the product as:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2 - 1) \\log_5(k+1)}{(k^2 - 4) \\log_5(k)} = \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k-1)(k+1) \\log_5(k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2) \\log_5(k)}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 4: Telescoping the Product\n\nNotice that the terms in the product telescope. Let's write out the first few terms to see the pattern:\n\\[\n\\frac{(4-1)(4+1) \\log_5(4+1)}{(4-2)(4+2) \\log_5(4)} \\cdot \\frac{(5-1)(5+1) \\log_5(5+1)}{(5-2)(5+2) \\log_5(5)} \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot \\frac{(63-1)(63+1) \\log_5(63+1)}{(63-2)(63+2) \\log_5(63)}\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63 \\cdot 64 \\cdot \\log_5(5)}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 64 \\cdot 66 \\cdot \\log_5(4)}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 5: Cancel Common Terms\n\nCancel the common terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63 \\cdot 64}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 64 \\cdot 66} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_5(5)}{\\log_5(4)} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\log_5(4)}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 6: Simplify the Fraction\n\nNotice that the terms in the numerator and the denominator form a product of odd and even numbers. We can write:\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 7: Final Simplification\n\nWe can simplify the fraction to:\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66}\n\\]\n\n#### Step 8: Final Answer\n\nThe final simplified form is:\n\\[\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 63}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot 66}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
